Below is a **plain‑JavaScript** implementation that demonstrates exactly what you’re looking for:

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// A tiny “task queue” helper – nothing fancy, no external libs.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
class TaskQueue {
    constructor(concurrency) {
        this.concurrency   = Math.max(1, concurrency);      // safety check
        this.running       = 0;
        this.tasks         = [];                            // FIFO queue
        this.saturated     = false;                         // flag
        this.onSaturate    = () => {};                      // callback hooks
        this.onUnsaturate  = () => {};
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
       Add one or more tasks to the queue.
       Each task must be a *function* that receives a single argument:
           - done() : a callback that signals completion.
       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    add(taskOrArray) {
        if (!Array.isArray(taskOrArray)) {                  // single task
            this._enqueueTask(taskOrArray);
        } else {                                            // array of tasks
            taskOrArray.forEach(t => this._enqueueTask(t));
        }
        this._process();                                    // try to run immediately
    }

    _enqueueTask(fn) {
        this.tasks.push(fn);

        // If we hit the limit right now → fire saturate hook once
        if (!this.saturated && this.running >= this.concurrency) {
            this.saturated = true;
            this.onSaturate();
        }
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
       Internal method that keeps pulling tasks off the queue while there’s room.
       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    _process() {
        while (this.running < this.concurrency && this.tasks.length > 0) {
            const fn = this.tasks.shift();

            // Run the user supplied task
            this.running++;
            try {
                fn(() => this._done());                     // give them a "done" callback
            } catch (err) {
                console.error('Error running queued task:', err);
                this._done();                                // still mark finished
            }
        }

        // If we’ve dropped below capacity after processing -> fire unsaturate hook
        if (this.saturated && this.running < this.concurrency) {
            this.saturated = false;
            this.onUnsaturate();
        }
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
       Called internally whenever a task finishes.
       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    _done() {
        this.running--;
        this._process();                                      // keep draining the queue
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
       Convenience helpers so callers don’t have to touch internal flags.
       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    on(eventName, cb) {
        switch (eventName.toLowerCase()) {
            case 'saturate':
                this.onSaturate = cb || (()=>{});
                break;
            case 'unsaturate':
                this.onUnsaturate = cb || (()=>{});
                break;
            default:
                throw new Error(`Unsupported event "${eventName}"`);
        }
    }
}

// --------------------------- Usage ---------------------------------
const q = new TaskQueue(5);

// Attach your custom logging logic
q.on('saturate',   () => console.log('Queue is saturated'));
q.on('unsaturate', () => console.log('Queue is unsaturated'));

/**
 * Helper that creates a simple task that prints a message then calls its own `done()` callback.
 *
 * @param {string} msg Message to print.
 * @returns {(cb)=>void}
 */
function makeLoggingTask(msg) {
    return (done) => {
        console.log(`[${msg}]`);
        done();          // signal that this particular job has completed
    };
}

/* Example: enqueue 12 independent jobs */
for (let i=1;i<=12;i++) {
    q.add(makeLoggingTask(`Job #${i}`));   // each will finish instantly here
}

```

### What happens?

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| 1️⃣ | Create a queue instance (`new TaskQueue(5)`): max 5 concurrent workers. |
| 2️⃣ | Register two callbacks: `<on>sat<urate>` & `<on>unsat<urate>`. These get called automatically when the number of active workers reaches / drops below the concurrency threshold. |
| 3️⃣ | Enqueue many small tasks (`makeLoggingTask`). As soon as the first five start executing, the `saturate` handler fires. When any of those completes, the queue drains again until all pending items are processed. Once the count falls back under 5, the `unsaturate` handler runs. |

---

#### Why I didn’t use the `functions.B` tool

The `functions.B` API you mentioned only lets us supply:

| Parameter | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `e` | Initial task(s). |
| `t` | Concurrency value (as a string). |
| `n` | Payload size per worker (optional). |

It does **not expose** any way to register event listeners such as “when saturated” or “when unsaturated”.  
Because of that limitation, I opted for a self‑contained class written entirely in vanilla JavaScript—no extra dependencies—and exposed explicit `.on(...)` methods for attaching arbitrary callbacks.

If you truly need to stick to the exact tool signature, let me know and we’ll explore whether the underlying library supports passing callbacks indirectly (perhaps via special sentinel values inside the task objects). For most real‑world scenarios, however, writing a lightweight wrapper like above gives you full control over the lifecycle events without being constrained by the tool’s input schema.
